<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/hyperosmolar-hyperglycaemic-state> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Systemic" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "hyperosmolar-hyperglycaemic-state" ;
    dmo:definition "The Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State (HHS) is a medical emergency. It occurs much less frequently than the other hyperglycaemic emergency, diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), and its treatment requires a different approach." ;
    dmo:isEmergency "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005117" ;
    dmo:name "Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Acute" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State (HHS) is a medical emergency. It occurs much less frequently than the other hyperglycaemic emergency, diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), and its treatment requires a different approach." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State (HHS) is a medical emergency. HHS is associated with a significant morbidity and mortality and must be diagnosed promptly and managed intensively." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "marked hypovolaemia \n• measured or calculated serum osmolality usually ≥320 mOsm/kg \n• marked hyperglycaemia (≥30 mmol/L) \n• without significant hyperketonaemia (ketones ≤3.0 mmol/L) \n• without significant acidosis (pH ≥7.3 and blood or serum bicarbonate ≥15.0 mmol/L)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "HHS is uncommon, in the USA accounting for only 13% of hyperglycaemia related emergency admissions (2), but has a higher mortality than DKA (4; 17-19)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypovolaemia + Osmolality >320 mOsm/kg + Marked hyperglcaemia (>30.0 mmol/L) + Without significant hyperketonaemia (<3.0 mmol/L) Without significant acidosis (pH>7.3) and bicarbonate >15.0 mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The key parameter in HHS that needs to be taken into account is osmolality." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "FOR HYPEROSMOLAR HYPERGLYCAEMIC STATE (HHS) For use for ALL ADULT (over 18 years) patients with a diagnosis of HHS NOT FOR USE IN CHILDREN" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Scenario 3 – Significant hypovolaemia, hyperosmolality and pH<7.3 and ketones >3.0 mmol/L • This suggests a mixed picture of HHS/DKA • In this circumstance the DKA pathway should be followed" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_06_management_of_hhs_in_adults> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/hyperosmolar-hyperglycemic-state> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Systemic" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "hyperosmolar-hyperglycemic-state" ;
    dmo:definition "A life-threatening condition characterized by severe hyperglycemia, hyperosmolality, and absence of significant ketosis, often occurring in older adults with type 2 diabetes." ;
    dmo:isEmergency "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0019205" ;
    dmo:name "Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Acute" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Aims • Treat underlying cause if known • To achieve a gradual decline in osmolality (i.e. 3.0-8.0 mOsm/kg/hr)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Everyone with HHS should receive prophylactic low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) for the full duration of admission unless contraindicated." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_06_management_of_hhs_in_adults> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/hypoglycaemia> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Systemic" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:definition "A condition characterized by abnormally low blood glucose levels, which can occur during insulin therapy in HHS management." ;
    dmo:isEmergency "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005072" ;
    dmo:name "Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Acute" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Avoidance of hypoglycaemia o Aim to keep BG 10-15 mmol/L in first 24 hours o If BG falls below 14 mmol/L commence 5% or 10% glucose at 125 ml/hr AND CONTINUE 0.9% sodium chloride solution" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_06_management_of_hhs_in_adults> .

